.TH TNT-CHAT 7 "2026-10-15" "TNT 1.8.0"
.SH NAME
tnt-chat \- use the TNT interactive terminal interface
.SH DESCRIPTION
The TNT interactive interface is a shared chat room presented through an
SSH terminal session.
By default it has no modes: typing types, and commands begin with
.BR / .
The
.B vim
keymap restores the modal interface described under
.BR "VIM KEYMAP" .
.P
Public messages are visible to the room and are persisted by the server.
Private messages are kept only in the participating sessions.
.SH CONNECTING
Connect with a terminal and request a PTY:
.P
.EX
ssh \-p 2222 any-user@chat.example.com
.EE
.P
The SSH login name controls authentication.
It does not select the interactive
nickname.
TNT prompts for a nickname after login.
An empty or invalid nickname becomes
.BR anonymous ,
or the first free
.BR anonymous2 ,
.BR anonymous3 ,
and so on when that is taken.
Nicknames are limited to 20 Unicode characters
and may not contain
.BR ✓ ,
.BR ✔ ,
.BR ☑ ,
or
.BR ✅ ,
which would pass for the verified mark described in
.BR "SIGNING IN" .
They must be unique among active
sessions without regard to ASCII letter case.
The names
.BR * ,
.BR system ,
.BR 系统 ,
and the
.B module:
prefix are reserved for internal messages.
A handle of a signed-in member is reserved too; see
.BR "SIGNING IN" .
.P
If the administrator configured an access token,
enter it as the SSH password.
The token is shared and does not authenticate the nickname.
.SH SIGNING IN
The room stays anonymous: anyone can connect and pick any free name.
A member of m1ngsama ID can also prove who they are, and their handle then
belongs to them.
.P
.B :login
starts the sign-in when the server runs an identity module.
The module shows what to do next, usually a link and a code.
Once it confirms the member, the session is renamed to the handle and
shows
.BI "Signed in as @" handle .
A session that was using that name, or one that looks like it, without
signing in is renamed to
.BR anonymous ,
or to the first free
.BR anonymous \fIN\fR,
and the room sees the rename.
The same happens when the identity module reserves a handle for a member
who signed in elsewhere.
Several sessions may be signed in to the same handle at once.
.P
TNT recognises a member by SSH key only when the SSH login name is their
handle:
.P
.EX
ssh \-p 2222 alice@chat.example.com
.EE
.P
Signing in from such a connection binds the key it authenticated with to
the handle.
From then on, connecting the same way with the same key signs the session
in at once, without the nickname prompt.
After signing in from a connection without a key, TNT says how to connect
next time; run
.B :login
once more from that connection to bind the key.
Under any other login name, nothing about the key is used.
.B :logout
unbinds the key, and the session becomes
.B anonymous
again.
.P
Nobody else can take a reserved handle, or a name that looks like one, at
the nickname prompt, with
.BR :nick ,
or as the login name of
.BR "exec post" .
Two names look alike when they are equal after TNT drops invisible
characters, combining accents,
.BR _ ,
.BR \- ,
.BR . ,
and spaces, reads fullwidth letters and common Cyrillic, Greek, and Latin
look-alikes as the ASCII letters they resemble, ignores ASCII letter case,
reads
.BR 0 ,
.BR 1 ,
.BR i ,
and
.B |
as
.BR o ,
.BR l ,
.BR l ,
and
.BR l ,
and reads
.B rn
as
.B m
and
.B vv
as
.BR w .
So
.BR m1ngsama ,
.BR MINGSAMA ,
.BR m1ng_sama ,
and
.B m1ngsarna
all look like
.BR mingsama .
m1ngsama ID applies the same rule when a member picks a handle.
The prompt falls back to
.BR anonymous ,
or to the first free
.BR anonymous2 ,
.BR anonymous3 ,
and so on, and both say:
.P
.EX
@alice is taken by a signed-in member, or looks like their name.
Use :login if it is you, or pick another name.
.EE
.P
Messages a signed-in member sends, public text and
.B /me
actions alike, show
.B ✓
after the sender's name, live and in history.
The mark has its own colour, never the theme's accent, and is bold in the
.B mono
theme.
.BR :users ,
.BR :last ,
and
.B :search
mark signed-in members the same way.
Since no nickname may contain a check mark, a name followed by
.B ✓
was signed in when it spoke.
TNT records the mark in
.I messages.log
when the message is sent; see
.BR tnt\-message\-log (5).
.SH KEYS
Typing inserts at the caret.
Nothing a user types moves the session into a different keyboard state.
.TP
.B Enter
Send the current nonempty message, or run a command when the line begins
with a recognised
.BR / word.
.TP
.B Ctrl-J, Alt-Enter
Insert a line break without sending.
.B Ctrl-J
is a literal line feed and works on every terminal;
.B Alt-Enter
depends on the terminal sending
.B Esc
before the carriage return, and is accepted only in this keymap, because
.B Esc
is how the vim keymap reaches NORMAL.
Pasted text keeps the line breaks it arrived with.
.TP
.B Esc
Dismiss the help or a command result.
It never changes what the keyboard does.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Clear the input.
When the input is already empty, TNT shows how to leave.
.TP
.B PgUp, PgDn
Scroll the room history without leaving the input.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move the caret between the lines of the message being composed.
While the input is empty they instead recall this session's last 16 sent
messages, so a message being composed is never replaced.
.SS Input region
The input region is one row tall and grows with the message, to at most six
rows.
Past that it scrolls and keeps the caret in view.
The room history shrinks by the rows the input region takes.
A message may be at most 1023 bytes as stored, and a line break costs two of
them; the counter that appears past 80 percent counts stored bytes.
.SS Commands
A line that begins with
.B /
and a recognised command word runs that command; the leading
.B /
is not part of it.
Write
.B //
to send a message that really begins with a slash.
Anything else, including a path such as
.IR /usr/local/bin ,
is sent as an ordinary message.
.SH VIM KEYMAP
The vim keymap is the modal interface: INSERT, NORMAL, and COMMAND, with
.B :
commands.
It is unchanged.
Enable it in any of three ways:
.TP
.B /vim
Switch this session.
.B /vim off
returns to the default keys.
.TP
.BI "ssh vim@" host
The SSH login name selects neither identity nor nickname, so it is free to
carry this request.
Put it in
.BR ssh_config (5)
once.
.TP
.B \-\-keymap vim
Make vim the default for a whole server.
See
.BR tnt (8).
.SS INSERT mode
INSERT mode edits and sends public messages.
.TP
.B Enter
Send the current nonempty message.
.TP
.B Ctrl-J
Insert a line break without sending.
.B Alt-Enter
is not accepted here, because
.B Esc
enters NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B Esc
Enter NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B Left, Right
Move the caret one character.
Text typed next is inserted at the caret.
.TP
.B Home, End
Move the caret to the start or the end of the display line it is on.
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the character before the caret.
.TP
.B Delete
Delete the character under the caret.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the word before the caret.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Delete the input line.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move through this session's last 16 sent messages.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete an online nickname, including an app user's, in the trailing
.B @
token.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Enter NORMAL mode.
.P
A caret movement never splits a character, an emoji, or a flag.
An escape sequence this build does not recognise is ignored; it does not
change modes.
.P
Bracketed multiline paste is inserted at the caret as one message, keeping
its line breaks.
A carriage return, alone or before a line feed, becomes one line break; a tab
becomes a space.
Input that exceeds the message limit or contains invalid UTF-8 is rejected
with a terminal bell.
.SS NORMAL mode
NORMAL mode browses the in-memory room history.
The view follows new messages until the user scrolls upward.
.TP
.B i, a, A, o, O
Enter INSERT mode.
.TP
.B :
Enter COMMAND mode.
.TP
.B /
Enter COMMAND mode with
.B search
preloaded.
.TP
.B j, Down
Scroll one line toward newer messages.
.TP
.B k, Up
Scroll one line toward older messages.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D, Ctrl-U
Scroll half a page down or up.
.TP
.B Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B
Scroll a page down or up.
.TP
.B PageDown, PageUp
Scroll a page down or up.
.TP
.B End, Home
Jump to the newest or oldest visible message.
.TP
.B g, G
Jump to the oldest or newest visible message.
.TP
.B ?
Open the interactive key reference.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Disconnect.
.SS COMMAND mode
COMMAND mode edits a command without the leading colon shown in command
documentation.
.TP
.B Enter
Execute the command.
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel the command and enter NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the preceding Unicode character.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the preceding word.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Delete the command line.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move through this session's last 16 commands.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete a command name.
For
.BR msg ,
.BR theme ,
and
.BR lang ,
it also completes the first argument; for
.B msg
that includes app users.
.SH MESSAGES
A public message must be valid UTF-8 and may contain at most 1023 bytes as
stored, where a line break costs two.
A line break is the only C0 or C1 control character accepted as message text;
every other one, and DEL, is refused.
.TP
.BI "/me " action
Send an action message.
The stored action includes the nickname.
It must fit the same message limit.
.TP
.BI @ nickname
Mention an online user in a public message.
The recipient receives a terminal bell and highlight.
.P
A message that is wider than the terminal wraps onto as many rows as it
needs, and the continuation rows align under the first row's text.
Wrapping never splits a character, an emoji, or a flag.
.SS Formatting
A small subset of markdown is rendered when the message is drawn,
in the message view and in the output of
.B :last
and
.BR :search .
The markers are ordinary printable characters and are removed from the
screen; the log keeps the text as it was typed.
.TP
.BI ** text **
Bold.
.TP
.BI \(ga text \(ga
Inline code.
Markers inside it are literal.
.TP
.B \(ga\(ga\(ga
On a line of its own, opens and closes a code block.
.TP
.BI http:// ...
An
.B http
or
.B https
URL is underlined where it stands.
.P
Headings, lists, tables, italics, and nesting are not rendered.
A marker that never closes stays literal, so ordinary text is never eaten.
Rendering adds no control character that the message itself may not
contain: the accepted input is unchanged.
.P
The room keeps the 100 most recent public messages in memory.
.P
The server owner may enable a moderation policy that rejects spam or
requests temporary source bans.
Anonymous and signed-in SSH sessions use the same policy interface.
See
.BR tnt (8)
for owner controls and private audit details.
Use
.B :last
or
.B :search
to read valid records from the active persistent log.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are introduced by
.B :
in NORMAL mode.
The following aliases have identical behavior.
.TP
.B :users, :list, :who
List active nicknames and connection durations.
Signed-in members are marked
.BR ✓ .
Users connected through an app follow, marked
.BR app .
The online count in the status bar includes them.
.TP
.BI ":msg " "user message"
.TQ
.BI ":w " "user message"
Send a private message to an active user, including one connected through
an app.
Both the sender and recipient receive a session-local inbox copy.
When an SSH user and an app user share a name, the SSH user gets the
message.
.TP
.BI ":reply " message
.TQ
.BI ":r " message
Reply to the most recent private-message peer.
.TP
.B :inbox
Open the private-message inbox, newest first.
Unread incoming messages are marked and counted until displayed.
The open inbox refreshes when a new private message arrives.
.TP
.B :inbox clear
Clear the inbox and the current reply target.
.TP
.BI ":nick " name
.TQ
.BI ":name " name
Change the current nickname.
A signed-in session must
.B :logout
first, and a handle another member signed in with is refused.
.TP
.B :login
Sign in with m1ngsama ID.
When the server has no identity module, it says that sign-in is not
available.
.TP
.B :logout
Sign out, unbind this session's SSH key, and become
.BR anonymous .
.TP
.BI ":last " [count]
Display up to
.I count
valid messages from the active log's recent record window.
The range is 1 through 50; the default is 10.
.TP
.BI ":search " keyword
Search persisted usernames and content without regard to ASCII letter case.
The last 15 matches are displayed.
Each occurrence of the keyword in the displayed text is highlighted, and
the highlight takes the place of any formatting under it.
.TP
.B :mute-joins, :mute
Toggle display of join and leave notices for this session.
.TP
.BI ":theme " [name]
.TQ
.BI ":color " [name]
Show or set the session colour theme.
Valid names are
.BR cyan ,
.BR green ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR blue ,
.BR amber ,
.BR red ,
and
.BR mono .
The default is
.BR cyan .
.TP
.BI ":lang " [en|zh]
.TQ
.BI ":language " [en|zh]
Show or set the session interface language.
A session starts in the language the SSH client sends in
.BR LC_ALL ,
.B LC_MESSAGES
or
.BR LANG ,
when it is English or Chinese, and in the server's language otherwise.
.TP
.B :help
Open the concise interactive manual.
.TP
.B :clear, :cls
Clear the current command output.
.TP
.B :q, :quit, :exit
Disconnect.
.SH PAGERS
Command output and help use a pager.
.TP
.B q, Esc
Close the pager.
.TP
.B j, k, Down, Up
Scroll one line down or up.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D, Ctrl-U
Scroll half a page down or up.
.TP
.B Ctrl-F, Space
Scroll a page down.
.TP
.B Ctrl-B, b
Scroll a page up.
.TP
.B PageDown, PageUp
Scroll a page down or up.
.TP
.B End, Home
Jump to the end or beginning.
.TP
.B g, G
Jump to the beginning or end.
.TP
.B r, R
Refresh live inbox output.
These keys have no effect in other command-output pages.
.TP
.B l, L
Cycle the interface language while the help page is open.
.SH CAVEATS
The private inbox holds at most 16 messages per session.
Older messages are discarded when it fills.
Private messages are not written to
.IR messages.log ,
do not appear in public history or exec output, and are lost at disconnect.
.P
A nickname that is not a signed-in handle is a label, not an identity.
Another session may reuse it after its owner disconnects.
A signed-in handle stays reserved after its owner disconnects or signs out.
.P
The terminal must support the ANSI control sequences used by the interface.
.SH EXAMPLES
Send an action from INSERT mode:
.P
.EX
/me waves
.EE
.P
Send and then answer a private message from COMMAND mode:
.P
.EX
:msg alice hello
:reply thanks
.EE
.P
Search persistent history:
.P
.EX
:search release
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR tntctl (1),
.BR tnt\-message\-log (5),
.BR tnt\-exec (7),
.BR tnt (8)
